::CW_U05_L02_GP_Q01::Draft sentence: Mara handed Lena the box after she dropped it, and it made her upset. Which revision is clearest?{
~A. She handed it over after it happened and it was upsetting.#Incorrect. This keeps the pronoun confusion.
~B. The box was there and it was a thing.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=C. After Maya opened the box, she handed the old photograph to Lena.#Correct. This revision names the people and object clearly so the reader knows who did what.
~D. They did it and then it happened.#Incorrect. This is even less clear.
}

::CW_U05_L02_GP_Q02::Draft: The rain stopped. I found the letter. The house was quiet. Which revision improves cohesion?{
=A. When the rain stopped, I found the letter on the quiet porch.#Correct. This combines the ideas smoothly and shows the relationship between events.
~B. The rain stopped. I found the letter. The house was quiet.#Incorrect. This repeats the choppy original.
~C. Letter quiet rain house found.#Incorrect. This removes sentence clarity.
~D. I found quiet because rain letter.#Incorrect. This is not coherent.
}

::CW_U05_L02_GP_Q03::Draft: i opened the door it was snowing Which revision fixes conventions?{
~A. i opened the door it was snowing.#Incorrect. Capitalization and sentence boundary issues remain.
~B. I opened the door it was snowing.#Incorrect. This still needs sentence separation or punctuation.
~C. opened door snowing i it was.#Incorrect. This is not standard word order.
=D. I opened the door. It was snowing.#Correct. This uses capitalization and sentence boundaries correctly.
}

::CW_U05_L02_GP_Q04::Draft: Jordan told Sam that he should leave because he was angry. What is the clarity problem?{
~A. The sentence has no characters.#Incorrect. Jordan and Sam are characters.
=B. The pronoun "it" is unclear because readers do not know what Jordan gave Sam.#Correct. This identifies the clarity problem in the sentence.
~C. The sentence has too many commas.#Incorrect. There are no commas.
~D. The sentence is already perfectly clear.#Incorrect. The pronoun reference is unclear.
}

::CW_U05_L02_GP_Q05::Which revision order is most effective for a creative draft?{
~A. Fix conventions first, then ignore meaning.#Incorrect. Meaning and structure should not be ignored.
~B. Add random punctuation before reading the draft.#Incorrect. That does not support revision.
=C. Check meaning and structure first, then polish conventions.#Correct. Larger revision decisions should come before final editing.
~D. Remove every creative choice.#Incorrect. Revision should preserve purposeful voice.
}
